{
  "anomaly_detected": true,
  "confidence": 0.95,
  "anomalies": [
    {
      "category": "bridged_nets",
      "components": ["U10", "U11"],
      "pins": ["U10.4", "U10.5", "U11.4", "U11.5"],
      "nets": ["GND"],
      "observed": "The isolated CAN transceivers U10 and U11 have both their logic-side ground (GND1, pin 4) and bus-side ground (GND2, pin 5) tied to the same GND net.",
      "expected": "For an isolated CAN transceiver, GND1 and GND2 must be on separate ground nets (e.g., GND and GND_ISO) to maintain galvanic isolation between the logic side and the CAN bus side.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U10\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"GND1\") (pintype \"power_in\")) (node (ref \"U11\") (pin \"5\") (pinfunction \"GND2\") (pintype \"power_in\"))"
        },
        {
          "source": "spec",
          "quote": "two isolated CAN buses (CAN0, CAN1), each driven by its own MCP2515 SPI CAN controller (U8, U9) through an isolated CAN transceiver (U11/companion)"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The galvanic isolation of both CAN buses is defeated, creating a common ground between the CM4 logic side and the CAN bus side. This can cause ground loops, noise, and loss of safety isolation.",
      "confidence": 0.95
    },
    {
      "category": "wrong_value",
      "components": ["R36"],
      "pins": ["R36.1", "R36.2"],
      "nets": ["VOUTN", "VOUTP"],
      "observed": "R36 is placed across the high-voltage PoE output nets VOUTP/VOUTN. Its value field is '24k', but its description and MPN (FRC0805J240TSD) indicate a 24Ω resistor.",
      "expected": "The component value and metadata must agree. A bleeder resistor across a high-voltage rail should be high value (e.g. 24k) to avoid excessive power dissipation; a 24Ω 0805 resistor would dissipate far beyond its rating.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(comp (ref \"R36\") (value \"24k\") ... (field (name \"MPN\") \"FRC0805J240TSD\") ... (field (name \"Description\") \" 125mW Thick Film Resistors 150V ±100ppm/℃ ±5% 24Ω 0805 Chip Resistor - Surface Mount ROHS\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "If the board is assembled using the MPN/description, a 24Ω resistor will be placed across the PoE output, causing excessive current and likely destroying the resistor or loading the PoE supply. If assembled as 24k, the BOM metadata is wrong and may mislead future builds.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    },
    {
      "category": "other",
      "components": ["D30", "R60"],
      "pins": ["D30.2", "D30.4", "R60.1", "R60.2"],
      "nets": ["ESTOP_OUT", "Net-(D30-K2)"],
      "observed": "D30 is a dual LED. Its second LED has anode A2 (pin 4) directly connected to ESTOP_OUT, while its cathode K2 (pin 2) is connected through R60 to the same ESTOP_OUT net. The LED and resistor form a loop with no voltage difference.",
      "expected": "A status LED should be connected between a supply rail and a controlled signal (or vice versa) so that it lights when the signal is in the desired state. Connecting both anode and cathode to the same net prevents the LED from ever illuminating.",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "netlist",
          "quote": "(node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"4\") (pinfunction \"A2\") (pintype \"input\")) (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"2\") (pintype \"passive\")) ... (node (ref \"D30\") (pin \"2\") (pinfunction \"K2\") (pintype \"input\")) (node (ref \"R60\") (pin \"1\") (pintype \"passive\"))"
        }
      ],
      "impact": "The ESTOP_OUT status indicator on D30 is non-functional; it will never light, so the board loses a visual indication of the e-stop output state.",
      "confidence": 0.9
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The design files contain multiple anomalies: the isolated CAN transceivers have their isolated grounds shorted together, R36 has a value/metadata mismatch that could lead to a damaging 24Ω resistor across the PoE output, and one LED of D30 is wired in a loop so it can never illuminate."
}

